\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {II}{\ignorespaces Stage-0 Representation Sufficiency Ablation on nuScenes under a fair 16-slot world-model context budget. All results are averaged over three seeds. Boldface marks the best fair-budget result in each column.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:repr_ablation}{{II}{6}{}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-B}}Stage-1 Cross-Dataset Imagination Policy Learning}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Stage-0 slot-type composition over validation samples, drawn directly from aggregate evaluation data. Under the fair 16-slot protocol, naive shared object-relation selection allocates too much of the compact context to relation tokens and starves nearby dynamic agents. The proposed typed-budget variants preserve dynamic coverage while still reserving a dedicated relation budget.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:slot_distribution}{{3}{7}{}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {III}{\ignorespaces Stage-1 imagination-based policy learning across datasets and nuPlan scales. All results are averaged over three seeds. Boldface marks the strongest return/safety value within each dataset block. Ego stability is reported as a divergence sentinel rather than the criterion for row ranking.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:stage1_cross_dataset}{{III}{7}{}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {IV}{\ignorespaces nuScenes Stage-1 diagnostic ablations under the same 700-scene protocol. These rows make explicit that neither a smaller relation budget nor a simple actor/critic fusion tweak rescues the default decoupled policy.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:nuscenes_aux_ablation}{{IV}{8}{}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Compact summary of the paper's data-driven evidence, drawn directly from the reported Stage-0, Stage-1, subset, and dataset-statistics aggregates. Panel (a) highlights the Stage-0 collapse of naive shared relation mixing and the recovery from typed-budget decoupling. Panel (b) shows the Stage-1 ranking reversal across nuScenes, nuPlan 20k, and nuPlan 50k. Panel (c) focuses on the interaction-heavy nuPlan subsets where WM-Decoupled-NoVis yields the largest collision reduction. Panel (d) summarizes the dataset-context differences that help explain why nuPlan makes relation structure more actionable.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:charts_placeholder}{{4}{8}{}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {\mbox  {VI-C}}Downstream nuPlan Planner-Like Sanity Check}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {V}{\ignorespaces NuPlan 50k offline planner-like sanity check. All results are averaged over three seeds. Lower action error indicates closer agreement with the teacher-derived planner action.}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:planner_sanity}{{V}{9}{}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {VII}Discussion}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {VI}{\ignorespaces NuPlan 50k interaction-conditioned subset analysis. Each subset isolates scenes where relation reasoning should matter most. All values are averaged over three seeds.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:interaction_subsets}{{VI}{10}{}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {VII}{\ignorespaces Support diagnostics beyond the main Stage-1 tables. Cross-dataset transfer rows are averaged over three seeds. Horizon-sensitivity rows come from the synchronized nuScenes follow-up sweep and are reported as direct diagnostic values rather than a new benchmark. Lower ego-cos step indicates smaller step-to-step ego-latent drift.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:support_diagnostics}{{VII}{10}{}{table.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {VIII}{\ignorespaces Support-only external and closed-loop sanity results. These rows are reliable and useful, but they should be interpreted as integration anchors rather than as main performance evidence.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:external_closed_loop_support}{{VIII}{10}{}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {IX}{\ignorespaces External PlanTF wrapper sanity details. The 13-scenario run disables official metrics for tractability, while the 1-scenario smoke confirms that official metrics can be produced but are not yet useful as benchmark evidence.}}{10}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:plantf_sanity}{{IX}{10}{}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Downstream offline evidence on nuPlan 50k, drawn directly from the planner-like sanity probe and the interaction-conditioned subset analysis. Panel (a) compares the two 50k finalists on teacher-action MSE, imagined return, and imagined collision. Panel (b) shows that the collision advantage of WM-Decoupled-NoVis is largest on subsets such as lane conflict, low TTC, dense agents, and the interaction union, which are exactly the regimes where relation-aware abstraction should matter most.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cross_dataset_placeholder}{{5}{11}{}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Qualitative slot-selection case under the fair 16-slot budget. In this sample, the naive Object+Relation-16 variant captures none of the five near-field dynamic agents within 15\,m and incurs 5.25\,m near-field prediction error. The proposed Obj+Rel-Decoupled variant captures all five and reduces the error to 0.24\,m, closely matching the high-context holistic reference while preserving the same compact budget as the naive model.}}{11}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:qualitative}{{6}{11}{}{figure.6}{}}
